<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikkō-ikki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ikko-ikki, Ikko leagues</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ikkō-ikki were leagues of farmers, local warriors, townspeople and priests belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in 15th- and 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In 1488 they overthrew the governor of Kaga and ruled the province until 1580, and from the fortified Ishiyama Hongan-ji they resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> for a decade before surrendering in 1580.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki.jpg" alt="Ikkō-ikki" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 投稿者がファイル作成 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turumaru_Flags.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>An ikki in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was any league whose members vowed to stand together for a single goal; warrior families and villagers formed many such leagues in the Muromachi period. The ikkō-ikki differed in being bound by faith in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a>, whose exclusive striving for rebirth in the Pure Land of Amitabha earned it the name ikkō, facing one direction. The leagues grew from the following of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a>, abbot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>'s Hongan-ji from 1457. When the warrior monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a> attacked his communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a> and destroyed Hongan-ji, Rennyo moved in 1471 to Yoshizaki in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echizen-province">Echizen Province</a>, which became a large fortified community.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Disputes among the local governors drew the Hongan-ji followers into fighting in the 1470s and 1480s, ending in the Kaga Rebellion, which overthrew the governor of Kaga and set up self-rule: the first time in Japanese history that commoners ruled a province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a> condemned the violence, but his successors Jitsunyo and Shōnyo organised the temples into a network that could raise troops quickly. The leagues fought for Hosokawa Masamoto in 1506, joined the Tenbun War of 1532-1536 and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> in 1536. Their strongholds included the fortress-temple of Ishiyama Hongan-ji outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, founded in 1496, and Nagashima on the border of Owari and Ise.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> defeated the leagues of Mikawa at Azukizaka in 1564. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> saw in them a rival that controlled the roads to the capital and allied with his enemies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a> and the Mōri, Azai and Asakura clans. He took Nagashima in 1574 after a four-year siege, and in 1575 ordered the people of Ajimano in Echizen killed. The 11th monshu, Kōsa, submitted with most of his forces, though a few held out until 1585.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The leagues drew on priests, peasants, merchants and jizamurai, local warrior landholders, and included warrior monks. Their arms were mixed: priests' robes and straw hats beside helmets and armour, naginata, swords and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanegashima">Tanegashima</a> matchlocks. They carried banners with slogans such as Namu Amida Butsu. The persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a> that followed led to the secret kakure nenbutsu societies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ikkō-ikki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the head priests Shonyo and Kennyo, Ishiyama became a moated fortress and temple town, supported by donations from believers across Japan and by the <b>Ikko leagues</b> that controlled provinces such as Kaga.</blockquote><a href="/state/ishiyama-hongan-ji">Ishiyama Hongan-ji</a></li><li><blockquote>Oda Nobunaga fought the <b>Ikko leagues</b> for years.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaga-ikko-ikki">Kaga Ikko-ikki</a></li></ul></section>
